  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1103 AM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSNOW SHOWERS TODAY:\n\nA fast-moving trough is making its way through the region today,\nbringing with it another round of active weather. Looking at \ncameras across the region, locations around and in the San Juans\nhave started to see impactful snow over the past several hours \nwith some areas sitting around 2-3 inches. Most roadways through\nthe San Juans are either wet or covered in snow at this point in\ntime, so expect for travel to be difficult at times should you\nbe driving through. By the time storms have moved out of\nsouthwestern Colorado, snow accumulations are anticipated to sit\naround 3-6 inches, though should banded snow develop, higher\naccumulations cannot be ruled out. The possibility of stronger\nsnow bands is low to moderate at this time though primarily due\nto lacking instability in areas northeast of the Four Corners. \nNonetheless, brief heavy snow rates remain in the realm of \npossibility. We've already seen visibility drop to a quarter \nmile in some areas due to snowfall, so even without heavy snow \nrates, reduced visibility may develop at several locations. Due \nto the already-present impacts across the San Juans combined \nwith increasing holiday travel, we have opted to issue a Winter\nWeather Advisory for the San Juans above 9,000 feet until 6PM \ntoday in spite of sub-advisory snow forecasts.\n\nThroughout the course of the day, look for the bulk of active\nweather to shift northeastwards into the central Colorado\nmountains. Some CAM's remain bullish on moderate snowfall rates\nmaking their way into the Sawatch Mountains and potentially over\nVail Pass, but we lean towards a middle-of-the-road outcome in\nregards to snow accumulations, with snow totals at and below\npass level around I-70 resting below 4 inches. Due to\nThanksgiving travel ramping up though, even this amount of snow\ncould cause issues during travel, especially given how impactful\nprecipitation has been on major roadways over the past couple\nweeks. Be weather aware and be prepared for tougher-than-normal\ntravel conditions today.\n\nANOTHER ROUND OF SNOW AFTER THANKSGIVING:\n\nA deep, yet fast ridge develops during the mid-week, bringing an\nend to precipitation region-wide. This precedes a late-week\nsystem passing through the western CONUS, with the likeliest\narrival sometime on Friday. Unfortunately, deterministic models\nare still struggling to come to an agreement on the nature of \nthis storm. What is apparent is the development of high pressure\noff the SoCal coast and split flow across the Intermountain \nWest. Where this split flow and resulting quieter weather occurs\nis highly uncertain at this point. Should it dip far enough \nsouth for strong northwesterly flow to establish itself over the\nCWA, we could see favorable orographics support snowfall over \nthe Park Range. This looks to be the most likely scenario at \nthis time, but with the upper-level pattern being so complex, \nthere is too little confidence to bank on this outcome coming to\nfruition. That being said, the probability of snowfall during \nthe late-week period is increasing, and so if you are planning \nto travel during this time, be prepared for the possibility of \ndifficult travel conditions, especially over the northern \nColorado mountains and maybe even across other portions of the \nCWA as well. Stay tuned over the coming days for additional \nupdates.",
